## GPO failure - RPC dynamic port range (2026-08-12)

### Symptom
gpupdate /force on CLIENT01 failed both Computer and User policy.
Console message: "Windows could not resolve the computer name."
Event 1055 (System log) fired fresh every policy cycle.

### Misleading part
The 1055 boilerplate blames name resolution / AD replication. It does not.
Same generic text covers several different underlying error codes.

### What was ruled out (all healthy)
- DNS: CLIENT01 points at 10.10.10.10, resolves landon.lab and DC01 fine
- Ports 389/445/88: Test-NetConnection all True
- SYSVOL: Test-Path \\landon.lab\SYSVOL = True
  (note: \\10.10.10.10\SYSVOL by IP fails by design - SYSVOL is DFS, accessed by domain name not IP)
- Secure channel: Test-ComputerSecureChannel = True
- Time: CLIENT01 syncing from DC01, no skew
- Single adapter, no dual-path / IPv6 routable
- Not a permissions issue: computer GP runs as SYSTEM (S-1-5-18)

### Root cause
Pulled real error code from the event:
(Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='System'; Id=1055} -MaxEvents 1).Properties.Value
Returned 1722 = "The RPC server is unavailable."

RPC is a two-step call:
1. Client hits RPC Endpoint Mapper on TCP 135 ("which port is service X on?")
2. Mapper returns a dynamic high port in 49152-65535
3. Client connects to that port for the actual work

MikroTik allow rule (VLAN 20 to VLAN 10) had dst-port ending in:
  49152,65535   <- comma = two individual ports
Needed:
  49152-65535   <- dash = the full range

135 was allowed so step 1 worked, but the random high port from step 2
was almost never 49152 or 65535 exactly, so the real RPC call was dropped.
Result: 1722, surfaced as the misleading "could not resolve computer name."

### Fix
WinBox > IP > Firewall > Filter Rules > the VLAN20-to-VLAN10 allow rule.
Dst. Port: change ...49152,65535 to ...49152-65535. Protocol tcp. Apply.
gpupdate /force on CLIENT01 then completed successfully.

### Lesson
Same class of bug as an earlier TCP 389 gap: symptom pointed at DNS,
cause was a missing firewall port. RPC needs 135 AND the full dynamic
range 49152-65535 as an actual range. When a GP error says "could not
resolve," pull the numeric code from the event Properties before trusting
the message.
